       "text": "<live-transcript>\n<passage id=\"P1\" document=\"Transcript · Meeting, 6 October\" file-modified=\"<date>\" location=\"0:00\">\n[0:00 Me] We agreed the enterprise price is 45 dollars a seat\n[0:00 Them] The budget is approved for this year\n[0:01 Me] Dana will send the contract by Friday\n[0:01 Them] The budget is approved for this year\n[0:03 Me] We agreed the enterprise price is 45 dollars a seat\n[0:03 Them] The budget is approved for this year\n[0:05 Me] Dana will send the contract by Friday\n[0:05 Them] The budget is approved for this year\n[0:07 Me] We agreed the enterprise price is 45 dollars a seat\n[0:07 Them] The budget is approved for this year\n[0:09 Me] Dana will send the contract by Friday\n[0:09 Them] The budget is approved for this year\n</passage>\n</live-transcript>\n\nThe transcript above is of a meeting the User just recorded: each line starts with its time and who spoke ([12:07 Them]). Me is the User, Them everyone else on the call.\nThe User took the notes below during the call, a line each. Fill them in from the transcript:\nReturn a ledger of facts, not separate prose accounts under each line and section. Code, not your reply, writes the full fact once and topic-only references with its original Citation in its other places. Do not write references or section drafts yourself.\n- Read through to the final agreement before allocating facts. Include all related final choices and work assignments under the User's ordinary lines, not just background or an earlier proposal. Never repeat or reword a line the User typed. A line asking about several things needs every relevant answer, with one identity per fact.\n- The User's lines are not the whole of the notes. The notes cover the whole call, as they would had the User typed nothing. A typed line collects only the facts that answer it; a fact that merely touches its subject, and every other decision, commitment, open question, figure and point of the call, is still written as a fact, with lines [] and its heading.\n- Each fact has one stable id, a short topic label, its complete wording once, its Citation marker inventory, and its related lines and sections. A fact is one short point, a single sentence: what a speaker said about several things is several facts. Keep an action, all its actual owners and its spoken deadline together, without restating the same owner or deadline within that fact. Write a decision once, as finally agreed, as one fact with every line and section it belongs to: never a second fact for the proposal that became it or for the same decision under another heading, and never its figures twice. A proposal that differs from what was agreed is a fact of its own. A first-person commitment belongs to that Transcript speaker, not automatically Me; a reported third-party assignment belongs to that third party.\n- Write the User, who reads these notes, in the first person (I, my; 我 in Chinese), even when the call gave their name, and as we or our (我们) where they spoke for their side. Name everyone else as they introduced themselves or were named in the call or in the User's notes, on either side: a named person is never the other side, 对方 or they, not even on the line where they give their name. When one named person is alone on the other side, every Them line is theirs, written with their name. When others are there too, named or not, a Them line may be anyone's: write a name only where the call makes clear who speaks that line, on the line itself or in what is said to them just before or after it; where it does not, never write a named person: a line plainly from across a call between two sides is the other side's (对方), and a line whose side is not clear has no speaker: write what was said, with someone (有人) where a sentence needs a subject. A name the User typed is written as they typed it: the transcript can mishear a name, so a heard name close to one the User typed is that person, in the User's spelling. Never invent a name. The speaker marks (Me, Them and numbered voices, or their translations) are not names: anyone else who was never named is the other side (对方) on a call between two sides, or in a recording of a room their numbered voice as marked.\n- The topic is a label only: no full assertion, figures, dates, owner, deadline or machine address. The complete wording belongs only in the paragraph after the metadata, not in the label or a second frame. Different facts may share a Citation, name or date; those are not reasons to merge their identities.\n- heading is the part of the call the fact belongs to, a short title the notes show above it, written word for word the same for every fact of that part: at most 6 headings in all, in the order the call took them up. Where the User typed a heading for that part, use its words. Like the topic, no figures, dates, owner or machine address.\n- lines lists related ordinary L addresses, most relevant first; use [] only when no ordinary line asks about this fact. A line that names the call as a whole, such as its title with who took part and what it was to cover, asks about no single fact: never list it, the notes below it cover the call. Include the final answer under its line even when a template section also needs the fact. Never put an H address in lines.\n- sections uses only these ids: \"decisions\" for final choices actually agreed, \"actions\" for actual commitments, a task someone in the call took on to do after it (not a plan or project they described), \"questions\" for questions explicitly raised and left unanswered; [] for anything else, which the notes show under its heading. Use multiple ids only when the same fact genuinely belongs to each. Do not turn a rejected option, hypothetical or undecided proposal into a decision, infer an open question, invent an owner/deadline, or turn a reported event into a promise.\n- claim is true only for a figure, date, price or term a document of the User's could state, such as a fee, a size or a contract term; never for someone's background, opinion, plan or task. Code checks the same full fact; do not write a second CLAIMS inventory. Overview metadata has only citations.\n- supersedes is always [] in this reply: it is only for replacing a fact kept from an earlier reply, which this fact corrects.\n- Before the first frame, write one control line naming every ordinary L line that nothing in the transcript above answers: UNANSWERED: L2 L5 is an example of the format, not a list to copy, and UNANSWERED: NONE when each line has something said to it. List a line only when it asks for something and nobody in the transcript speaks to it at all. A line answered in part, or answered by someone saying it is not known or not yet decided, is answered: write that as its fact. Never list a heading, or a line that names the call as a whole. Write no fact saying that something was not said or not given: the app writes that under a listed line.\n- Only a structural heading, never an ordinary paragraph, can host a section. A heading hosts the section its words ask for, in any wording or language, whether or not the User typed lines under it: SUMMARY for what the call was about overall, DECISIONS for final choices, ACTIONS for commitments and their owners, QUESTIONS for questions left open, CLAIMS for what was said that the User's Documents should be checked against. NONE is only for the Note's title or a heading that names a subject of the meeting (a topic, a person, an agenda item) rather than one of those kinds of content.\n- Begin the reply with one control line assigning EVERY H heading: HEADINGS: H1=ACTIONS H2=NONE is an example of the format, not a mapping to copy. Use only the H addresses actually listed below and choose their actual section by what each heading's words ask for, as above. Each section can use at most one heading and each heading at most one section. These assignments are machine routing, never prose. Do not assign a general topic heading to an unrelated section.\nUse new ids P1_F1, P1_F2 and so on. Each closed fact frame has exactly this shape; replace the example's labels, addresses, sections and text with the actual fact, not its topic repeated in every place:\n### FACT P1_F1\n```json\n{\"topic\":\"<short topic only>\",\"heading\":\"<the part of the call>\",\"citations\":[1],\"lines\":[],\"sections\":[\"actions\"],\"claim\":true,\"supersedes\":[]}\n```\n<One complete fact, including its actual owner and deadline if said.> [^1]\n### END P1_F1\n- Keep metadata inside its closed json fence; Citation markers appear only in the ordinary paragraph outside it. citations lists every marker required by that paragraph. No extra keys, nested headings, code or extra paragraphs in a fact. Close every frame with its matching END heading.\nAlways include one separately cited overview: two or three short sentences on what the call was about and what came out of it, naming outcomes in general words (a venue was chosen, not which one), never the figures, dates or owners the notes below give, and not commentary about these notes. A filled-out agenda line is not a substitute. Use this closed frame:\n### OVERVIEW\n```json\n{\"citations\":[2,3]}\n```\n<What the call was about.> [^2] <What came out of it.> [^3]\n### END OVERVIEW\n- Every sentence and bullet ends with its citation marker, citing the transcript line it comes from.\n- Write only what the transcript says. Leave out what it doesn't.\n- Write in English, the language of the call.\n- The transcript's marks are not names. Someone never named is written with their mark's word in the language you write in, Them's only on a call between two sides: in English Me is I, Them is the other side, and a numbered voice keeps its mark (Speaker 2); in Simplified Chinese Me is 我, Them is 对方, Speaker ? is 发言人 ?, Speaker 2 is 发言人 2. Never call the User \"the User\" or 用户.\n- The other side (对方) is only for a call between two sides: someone across who was never named, or a line from across that cannot be put on one person. On a call among the User's own team, or where it is not clear the call has two sides or which side a line came from, nobody is the other side: a line nobody can place has no speaker.\n- Each Citation quote must be contiguous words from one transcript line, without its time or speaker label. When the words occur more than once, set the cite record's location to the timestamp of the occurrence you mean.\n- Then, in the same reply, call cite once with a record for every marker in the notes.\n\nThe notes:\nH1 (heading): Pricing\nL1: price\n\nFill in my notes from the call, and write the rest of the call’s notes."
